WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.737
and Golden Globe Award-winning
actor you know from the hit

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:07.940
TV series "Fargo"
and films such as "Sling Blade,"

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.909
"Bad Santa,"
and "Friday Night Lights."

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:12.478
He stars in the fourth and final
season of "Goliath,"

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:14.781
which is now streaming
on Amazon Prime Video.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:16.015
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:17.816
-Let's put this to bed
for $10 million.

00:00:17.816 --> 00:00:19.719
-Zax and Tillinger
and Russell Drug

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.054
flooded this country
with billions of pills.

00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:23.589
You get them hooked,
and you sell them more.

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.791
People die.
Who gives a [bleep]?

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:29.229
You can always hook a new
customer with a bad back.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:31.297
You know,
George Zax had the balls

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:34.734
to give out free 30-day
prescriptions to new patients.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.069
-Zax Pharma has settled.

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.870
-Yeah, not on my watch.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.739
Your clients
have been reckless.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.176
They killed over 200,000 people
for profit.

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.578
That's a targeted business plan.

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.480
Now, I may be new here.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.682
Maybe I'm just
getting up to speed.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:52.218
But I'm in the mood
to stick my fist up your ass

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.454
and pull out billions.
You get it?

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:57.790
-Please welcome back to the show
Billy Bob Thornton.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.827
How are you, friend?
-Hey, what's up, Seth?

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.529
-That's a hell of a clip
they sent along.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:04.930
Thank you very much for that.
-Yeah.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.468
Oh, that's just one
of many, many gems.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:11.403
-So I obviously this has been
a difficult couple of years

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:14.140
for a lot of people,
but I did not know that you had

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.644
this added negative element
that you are a germaphobe,

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:20.613
which must have made it
especially hard.

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:24.083
-Yeah, yeah.
I've always been that way.

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.886
I've got, you know,
a few phobias.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:32.891
And I really didn't change
when the pandemic came along.

00:01:32.891 --> 00:01:36.729
I was already doing all
that stuff anyway, you know?

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.165
It's like, you know,
wash your hands constantly.

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:41.968
I already do. So, yeah.
Worked out nice.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.236
-You're one of those people

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.572
who already had gloves
at your house?

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:46.805
-Oh, absolutely.

00:01:46.805 --> 00:01:49.475
Hazmat suits. Everything.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.944
-So, one thing that you were
able to do at home,

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:54.280
which must have made it
a little bit nicer --

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.017
You're also a musician.
You have a band.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:00.719
And is it true that you recorded
four albums

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:02.922
since this thing started?

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.757
-Three, yeah.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:10.162
We did three albums,
which was pretty nice

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:13.499
because when we went
to the recording studio,

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:17.636
we just, you know -- it was
kind of a bubble there also.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:21.174
And, yeah, so we have three
new Boxmasters albums,

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:22.741
including a Christmas album

00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:24.910
that we're putting out
in November,

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.046
which is not your typical
Christmas album.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:30.683
It's not "Let it snow,
let it snow", you know?

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:34.219
It's a little darker,
a little sadder,

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:37.089
you know, than most
Christmas albums might be.

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:38.557
-Well, I think we'd expect

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:41.260
nothing less from Bad Santa,
you know?

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.928
-Exactly, yeah.

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:47.333
-Now, is that Captain Beefheart
behind you?

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.203
-That is absolutely
Captain Beefheart, yes.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:52.572
-Do you have a lot
of your favorite photos

00:02:52.572 --> 00:02:54.674
of your favorite artists
on the wall at your home,

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:58.277
or is he a special exception?

00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:01.314
-Well, no, I'm actually
in the recording studio

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.516
right now, in the lounge
of the recording studio

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:06.185
that we have out here
in California.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.821
And I was friends

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:12.358
with a famous photographer
named Jim Marshall.

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:17.263
And Jim photographed, you know,
everybody back in the day,

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:19.865
and he was quite a character.

00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:24.270
And I have a collection
of Jim's photos here.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.273
And we had a deal, Jim and I.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:32.578
He would sell me one,
which weren't cheap, and --

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:36.348
He would sell me one,
and he would give me one.

00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:37.884
So that was really cool.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:40.219
And he knew that I was a big fan

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.355
of Frank Zappa
and Beefheart and all them.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.424
And so he would always give me
their photos

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:47.026
if I would buy a Janis Joplin
or whatever.

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:50.362
-I read an article about
one of my favorite artists,

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:52.465
Warren Zevon,
that talked about a time

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:55.234
that he was --
just stopped by your studio

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.669
and was just hanging out

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.471
and actually recorded
some stuff there.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:01.407
Was he someone that you were
lucky enough to know long-term,

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.709
or was that just
a one-time thing?

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.646
-No, I knew Warren
since the late '80s.

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:10.816
When I was -- I was living
in an apartment building

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:14.320
on Kings Road in L.A.
in West Hollywood.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.255
And Warren was my neighbor.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.124
He lived in the building, too.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:21.160
And -- You know how
in apartment buildings

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.163
you have this bank of mailboxes?

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.464
You go and check your mail

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:27.433
before you go up
to your apartment.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.235
And the first time I met Warren,

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:30.903
we didn't meet through
the entertainment business.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.205
We met at the mailbox.

00:04:33.205 --> 00:04:38.511
And I have severe OCD,
and so did Warren.

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:43.315
And he was standing next to me
checking his mail,

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:46.619
and I opened my mailbox
and pulled the mail out,

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:49.021
put it back in, closed it,
opened it again.

00:04:49.021 --> 00:04:51.857
I did that three times,
and he was watching.

00:04:51.857 --> 00:04:55.261
And he goes,
"You have it, too, huh?"

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:59.164
And I go, "Yeah, I do."
So then we got to be friends.

00:04:59.164 --> 00:05:04.203
And it became a competition
on who had the worst OCD.

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:05.471
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:09.409
And, by the way,
as bad as mine is, he won.

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:12.911
And so...

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:16.882
But he ended up,
on his last album,

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:18.985
you know,
before he passed away.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.254
He used to come
to the house a lot,

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:22.755
and we hung out quite a bit.

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:27.493
And he recorded about three
of the songs for his last album

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:31.230
in my old studio
that I had bought from Slash.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:36.034
And he actually did
"Knockin' on Heaven's Door"

00:05:36.034 --> 00:05:38.203
in my studio,
which was pretty chilling,

00:05:38.203 --> 00:05:40.373
because, you know,
Warren knew he was dying.

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:44.009
And when he recorded that
in the studio,

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:46.178
I'll never forget
just sitting there listening.

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:49.414
I did background vocals
with a couple of other guys

00:05:49.414 --> 00:05:51.450
on that song with him.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.552
And -- It was pretty chilling.

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:55.154
-Yeah, I mean, I remember,
you know,

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:57.222
that when that album came out
and listening to him

00:05:57.222 --> 00:05:58.791
sing that song,
and, obviously, you were aware

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:01.461
that he knew
what his diagnosis was.

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:03.930
So I can only imagine,
as sort of haunting

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.298
as it was to listen to it
off the album,

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:09.235
I can't imagine what it was like
to record it with him.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:11.770
What a piece of history that is.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.338
-No, it was amazing.

00:06:13.338 --> 00:06:16.175
And I remember that night,

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.611
Warren had a silver ring
that he wore.

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:21.881
It was just kind of
a square silver ring.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.082
Didn't have any markings on it
or anything.

00:06:24.082 --> 00:06:26.752
And he said, "I've worn
this ring for a long time."

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.488
And he said,
"I want you to have it."

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:31.957
And Warren had
a very dark sense of humor.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:34.827
And he handed it to me,

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:37.563
and I held it there,

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:40.232
and he looked at me and he said,

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:42.268
"You're not gonna wear it,
are you?"

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:44.636
I said, "No, of course not."

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:47.073
And he goes,
"I knew you wouldn't."

